-- title:
--   A lift of a fibre uniformiser is an étale coordinate
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and a discrete valuation ring, and let $\varpi \in A$ generate its maximal ideal, i.e. $\mathfrak m_A = (\varpi)$. Let $S$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and local, and let $i : A \to S$ be a ring homomorphism which is formally smooth and essentially of finite type, and such that the composite of $i$ with the residue map $S \to S/\mathfrak m_S$ is surjective (so the closed point of $S$ is rational over the residue field of $A$). Let $t \in S$ be an element such that the maximal ideal of $S$ is generated by the two elements $i(\varpi)$ and $t$, and such that $t$ does not lie in the ideal of $S$ generated by $i(\varpi)$. Then the evaluation homomorphism $A[X] \to S$ determined by $i$ on coefficients and by $X \mapsto t$ is formally smooth, formally unramified, and essentially of finite type.
--
--   This is the assertion that, at a residually rational point of a formally smooth, essentially finite type local $A$-algebra of relative dimension one over a discrete valuation ring, any element $t$ lifting a uniformiser of the closed fibre is an étale coordinate: $A[X] \to S$, $X \mapsto t$, is formally étale in the essentially finite type sense. It is used to produce étale coordinates in the Krull dimension at most one setting and, on semistable models of curves, at points of the smooth locus over a discrete valuation ring.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_RingHom_formallySmooth_and_formallyUnramified_eval2RingHom_of_maximalIdeal_eq_span_pair.lean

